====================================================================================================
ORTHOGONAL DESIGN vs RANDOM HYPERCUBE
====================================================================================================
  n = 15 parameters, T = 8192 shots, R = 0.45, readout var = 1.0,
  300 repeats. Hadamard order D = 16.

  REGISTER WIDTH:   random 15 qubits   ->   Hadamard 4 qubits   (11 saved)

  Aliasing structure: column i times column j is column (i XOR j), so a
  degree-2 term on (i,j) lands on the main effect of (i XOR j) whenever
  that column is in use. Random sigma has no such term at any order.

  Shot allocation across design rows is BALANCED (exactly T/D per row).
  Random allocation leaves an O(1/sqrt(T)) imbalance that restores the
  cross term and erases the whole effect; the last block below shows that.

   deg-2 wt  |bias| rnd  |bias| had    var rnd    var had    MSE rnd    MSE had   cos rnd   cos had   winner
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
       0.00     0.00198     0.00091    0.00249    0.00060    0.00249    0.00060    0.9990    0.9997 Hadamard
       0.10     0.00152     0.09286    0.00206    0.00061    0.00206    0.01250    0.9989    0.9928   random
       0.25     0.00168     0.23635    0.00132    0.00061    0.00132    0.07451    0.9982    0.8831   random
       0.50     0.00307     0.42944    0.00390    0.00060    0.00390    0.27313    0.9988    0.9543   random
       1.00     0.00402     1.14193    0.00591    0.00061    0.00591    2.13054    0.9983    0.7216   random

  ALLOCATION CHECK, at zero degree-2 weight so nothing aliases.
    balanced rows   var = 0.000594
    random rows     var = 0.003075
    random sigma    var = 0.002957
    predicted with cross term    (C + v/R^2)/T = 0.003029
    predicted without cross term (v/R^2)/T     = 0.000603
    Balanced allocation should shed C = sum_{j!=i} g_j^2 and keep only the
    readout term. If it does not separate from the other two, the
    orthogonality argument is wrong rather than merely mis-implemented.

  The first row has NO degree-2 content, so it isolates the orthogonality
  gain with nothing aliasing: any Hadamard advantage there is the cross-term
  cancellation alone. Later rows add the aliasing the saving pays for.

====================================================================================================
RESOLUTION IV BY FOLDOVER:  does one extra qubit remove the aliasing?
====================================================================================================
  Design plus its sign reversal: 32 rows, 5 register
  qubits against 15 for the random hypercube. Main effects become
  clear of every two-factor interaction, so the bias should vanish while the
  variance gain is kept.

   deg-2 wt  |bias| rnd   |bias| IV    var rnd     var IV    MSE rnd     MSE IV   cos rnd    cos IV   winner
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
       0.00     0.00277     0.00083    0.00298    0.00061    0.00298    0.00061    0.9991    0.9998 foldover
       0.10     0.00107     0.00105    0.00245    0.00061    0.00244    0.00061    0.9990    0.9997 foldover
       0.25     0.00136     0.00100    0.00150    0.00061    0.00149    0.00061    0.9985    0.9994 foldover
       0.50     0.00167     0.00111    0.00309    0.00061    0.00309    0.00061    0.9987    0.9997 foldover
       1.00     0.00351     0.00080    0.00473    0.00059    0.00473    0.00059    0.9974    0.9997 foldover

  If the bias column stays flat while the variance gain holds, the register
  drops from n to log2(n)+1 with no accuracy cost, the block-width cap lifts,
  and the 2n advantage grows with n rather than sitting at the qubit limit.

  If Hadamard holds up to realistic degree-2 weight, the block-width cap
  lifts and the 2n advantage grows with n instead of sitting pinned at the
  register limit. If it does not, the register saving is real but bought
  with a bias the random design never has, and resolution IV at twice the
  rows is the next thing to price.
